WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 99 for July 11th, 2026.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.460
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.920
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.400
Let's dive in. You know that feeling when a single photo makes you wish you could step inside

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:25.680
and look around the corner? Google just shared a research prototype called Project Genie that turns

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.340
real street view photos into full interactive 360-degree worlds you can walk through.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.200
Think of it like taking one snapshot from your vacation and having the system

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.580
instantly build the entire block behind it, beside it, and ahead of it.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:45.960
The tool starts with an actual location from Google Maps street view so the first view

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:52.080
already feels grounded in reality. Then it uses your movements to decide what comes next.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.700
Almost like the environment is responding to where you choose to go.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:01.520
One helpful way to picture this is to choose your own adventure book where every page is generated

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:07.780
the moment you turn it instead of being printed in advance. Another angle is a video game level

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:13.280
that builds itself as you play, using real streets as the starting map rather than an artist

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:20.000
drawing everything beforehand. This matters because most virtual worlds either cost huge teams months

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.360
to create or feel empty once you start exploring. With Project Genie a student could stand on their

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:32.000
own neighborhood street and turn it into an underwater scene for a history project or a puzzle game.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:37.760
A creator could take a famous landmark and experiment with different weather or time periods

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:44.440
without any game design experience. Right now it is still a research prototype so no public demo

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:51.080
exists yet. Keep an eye on the Google Deep Mind and Google Labs accounts on X for early examples

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:57.260
the team has been sharing. Okay now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive into how something

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:03.160
like this actually works. You already know how your phone keyboard guesses the next word while you

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:10.060
text, making the sentence feel smooth even though it only predicts one piece at a time. Project Genie

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.840
uses the same kind of guessing except it works with pictures instead of words. It looks at the

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.760
current view, checks the direction you are moving, and predicts what the next frame should show.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:27.120
It repeats this prediction for every new frame so the world stays consistent even when you turn around

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:33.140
or explore behind the starting camera angle. The first frame comes from a real street view photo,

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:38.820
which gives the system a solid anchor instead of starting from nothing. Because it builds the scene

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:45.160
one frame at a time rather than trying to create an entire finished world at once. Your choices like

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:51.140
swimming or climbing can change what appears right away. The model learned patterns from huge amounts of

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:57.420
video, so lighting, textures, and movements stay believable as you move through the space. The result

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:04.140
feels alive precisely because each second is freshly imagined based on what you do next. That step by

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:09.280
step guessing process is what turns a flat photo into something you can actually explore.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.980
Now let's look at some other interesting tools you can try today. First up is an eco-friendly chatbot

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:22.280
called EcoGPT that answers questions while using far less computing power than most other models.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:28.380
Think of it like a smaller engine that still gets you where you need to go but burns less fuel along the

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:35.280
way. The team has already used savings from this approach to help plant over 34,000 trees through

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:42.240
two charities. You can try it right now by searching for EcoGPT chatbot, opening the site, and

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:48.000
starting a conversation. Type something simple like, tell me a short story about a robot that

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.940
recycles. And notice how the answers feel natural while the system runs on roughly one tenth the

00:03:53.940 --> 00:04:00.660
energy of typical chatbots. Next is a researcher testing an AI called Codex on the daily challenge

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:06.940
in the game Slay the Spire. The model has already beaten the first boss on a tough randomized run,

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:11.960
showing how these tools can handle strategy games where rules change each time.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:18.240
If you play similar games you can experiment yourself by opening chat GPT or Claude and

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.860
describing a simple game scenario to see how it reasons through choices. That gives you a quick

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:29.740
way to watch an AI think through decisions without needing any coding skills. Here are a couple of

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:37.080
quick bits worth knowing. When a new model claims 80% on a test it usually means the AI got 80% of the

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:43.300
questions right on a specific set of problems. This percentage comes from how many answers matched the

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:49.840
expected correct responses in that benchmark. The test itself is a fixed collection of questions or

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:56.800
tasks designed to evaluate certain skills. Even a perfect score would not mean the model is finished or

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:02.520
ready for everything. New and harder tests keep getting created by researchers to push the limits

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:08.940
of what we can measure. These numbers give a useful comparison point between different models on the same

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:15.080
tasks. They do not capture every possible real world situation the AI might face later.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:22.280
On another note, roughly half of the Fortune 500 companies now use open models and data sets shared

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:28.980
on the hugging face platform. The platform allows anyone to access these models and data sets freely.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.900
This adoption by large companies shows how open source approaches are gaining traction quickly.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:40.480
Students and hobbyists benefit because they can download and experiment with the same tools.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:47.500
No payment is required to get started with many of the shared resources. The CEO of hugging face has

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:53.700
highlighted that this trend supports broader innovation in the field. It opens doors for more people to

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:59.960
learn about AI without financial barriers. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go,

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:06.520
huh, that's cool. Go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow. And if

